/* Authored study-session through-lines (roadmap piece c). One paragraph per
   Schweser session or curated cluster, keyed by studyPath block id: what the
   block covers, its key points, and how it connects to the overall narrative.
   The Block Review overview renders this where present (else it composes one
   from deps via ReadingArc), and it doubles as the case-study (E) narrative
   spine. Every fact is grounded in the readings' own sourced material (the
   tags/summaries in meta-data.js). Written functionally in Phase 2; flagged for
   the Phase 5 human-tone pass. No em/en dashes. */
export const throughlines = {
  "b1-1": "This block builds the market-risk measurement toolkit that every later book reuses. Reading 1 defines VaR and Expected Shortfall from first principles, the ruler everything else calibrates against. Readings 2 and 3 sharpen how you estimate that ruler: Reading 2 makes historical simulation smarter with bootstrapping, weighting, and filtering, and Reading 3 models the tail directly with extreme value theory when the ordinary distribution runs out. Reading 4 then audits the number you produced, counting exceptions against Kupiec and the Basel traffic-light zones, because a risk figure nobody backtests is just a guess. Reading 5 scales the idea to a real book by collapsing thousands of positions onto a handful of risk factors. Reading 6 closes with the academic verdict: VaR is not subadditive, and liquidity and market integration matter. Learn this block well, because credit VaR, operational capital, and liquidity-adjusted VaR in the later books are all applications of the machinery built here.",
  "b1-7": "This block treats correlation not as a statistic but as a risk in its own right. Reading 7 makes the case: wrong-way risk, correlation-sensitive products, and the 2008 crisis all turn on how assets move together, not just how each moves alone. Reading 8 looks at how correlation actually behaves in the data, where it clusters into regimes, mean-reverts, and is far from constant. Reading 9 builds the tool for stitching individual behaviors into a joint distribution, the copula, and explains why the Gaussian copula's thin tails made it fail exactly when correlations spiked. The through-line: correlation is dangerous precisely because it is unstable and rises under stress, and the models that assumed otherwise broke. This sets up the credit-correlation and copula machinery you meet again across Book 2.",
  "b1-10": "This block moves from estimating risk to modeling the interest-rate world that drives it. Reading 10 starts empirically, using regression hedging and principal components to decompose curve moves into level, slope, and curvature. Reading 11 lays the pricing foundation with binomial trees, backward induction, and risk-neutral valuation, the science every rate model runs on. Reading 12 explains why the curve takes the shape it does through expectations, convexity, and the risk premium. Readings 13 and 14 are the heart of the block: a nested family of drift models (Model 1, Model 2, Ho-Lee, Vasicek), then volatility models (time-decaying volatility, CIR, lognormal), each fixing a shortcoming of the one before. Reading 15 reads the market's own view of the distribution out of the volatility smile. Reading 16 is the regulatory capstone, FRTB, where Expected Shortfall replaces VaR, liquidity horizons enter the capital charge, and every trading desk faces its own tests. The arc: build the rate model, then watch the regulator turn risk measurement into required capital.",
  "b2-17": "This block builds the credit-risk vocabulary and process before any model appears. Reading 17 fixes the terms every later reading uses: obligor, exposure, and the PD, LGD, and EAD that combine into expected loss, plus the unexpected loss that capital exists to cover. Readings 18 and 19 put that inside an institution, who owns credit risk through policies, limits, and committees, and how the credit process runs from origination through monitoring to workout. Reading 20 makes the pivotal distinction concrete: expected loss is a cost you price in, unexpected loss is what capital absorbs. Reading 21 then maps the modeling terrain ahead, structural versus reduced-form versus empirical, and Readings 22 to 24 populate it with scoring and rating models and their validation, retail scorecards and cutoffs, and sovereign country risk. The arc: name the pieces of a loss, decide who manages them, then choose a model to size them.",
  "b2-25": "This block is about estimating the inputs credit VaR needs, then pricing credit directly. Reading 25 gives three independent routes to a default probability: read it off ratings, back it out of market spreads as a risk-neutral figure, or derive it structurally from the Merton model. Reading 26 assembles those into credit VaR, where losses are skewed, horizons are long, and the unexpected-loss-versus-expected-loss split matters more than in market risk. Reading 30 turns to credit derivatives, the CDS mechanics, pricing, and indices that let you trade or hedge the credit risk you just measured. The through-line: estimate default probability, turn it into a loss distribution, then trade the exposure.",
  "b2-31": "This block is the counterparty-risk arc from first exposure to a priced-in charge. Reading 31 locates where counterparty risk lives, in OTC derivatives and securities financing, and Reading 32 names the triad that defines it: exposure, default probability, and wrong-way risk. Readings 33 to 35 are the mitigation toolkit, netting to compress exposure, collateral and margin to cover it, and central clearing to mutualize it while concentrating it in the CCP. Reading 36 measures the exposure itself through the EE, PFE, and EPE profiles, and Reading 37 prices that exposure into the trade as CVA, with DVA and wrong-way effects. Reading 38 stresses both the exposure and the CVA, and Reading 39 steps to securitization, the SPV-and-waterfall machine where the 2008 credit crisis broke. The arc: find the exposure, shrink it, measure it, price it, stress it.",
  "b3-40": "This block builds the operational-risk framework end to end. Reading 40 defines what counts as operational risk and argues that resilience, absorbing shocks, matters more than trying to prevent every one. Reading 41 sets the governance: three lines of defense, risk appetite, and culture. Readings 42 and 43 are the analytical core, identifying risks through RCSAs, scenario workshops, and taxonomies, then measuring them by frequency and severity with scenario quantification. Reading 44 covers mitigation, the accept, avoid, mitigate, transfer menu plus controls and insurance, Reading 45 covers reporting through KRIs and dashboards, and Reading 46 integrates the risk types into one framework. The arc: define op risk, govern it, find it, size it, treat it, report it.",
  "b3-47": "This block applies the operational-risk framework to the modern threat surface, largely through case studies. Readings 47 and 48 cover cyber resilience and a real breach where the controls failed; Reading 49 covers internal and external fraud mechanics. Readings 50 and 51 make one point twice: you can outsource an activity but not the risk, and vendors can become your biggest vulnerability. Reading 52 covers mis-selling and compliance failures. Readings 53 and 54 elevate models themselves to a risk class under SR 11-7, with an inventory, validation, and effective challenge, and a case where the model was the operational failure. Reading 55 closes with supervisory stress testing and the CCAR logic that ties scenarios to capital trajectories. The through-line: the same identify, measure, control discipline aimed at cyber, fraud, third parties, and models in turn.",
  "b3-56": "This block is about how much capital a bank must hold and why. Reading 56 sets the economic-capital logic, RAROC and the attribution of the diversification benefit. Readings 57 and 58 show how banks build economic-capital frameworks and how supervisors expect capital planning to work. Reading 59 traces the regulatory history, Basel I to Basel II, the three pillars, and what went wrong before 2008. Readings 60 to 62 are Basel III: higher-quality capital, buffers, a leverage ratio, and the LCR and NSFR liquidity rules, then the 2017 finalization with its output floor and revised approaches, and finally the standardized operational-risk capital charge built from the business-indicator component and internal loss multiplier that replaced AMA. The arc: decide capital economically, then watch regulation tighten it crisis by crisis.",
  "b4-63": "This block builds liquidity risk from definition to failure. Reading 63 splits funding liquidity from market liquidity and introduces liquidity-adjusted VaR and the liquidity spiral. Reading 64 shows how leverage manufactures liquidity risk on both sides of the balance sheet, and Reading 65 covers the early-warning signals that a run is coming. Readings 66 to 69 are the management toolkit: why banks hold securities, asset-versus-liability liquidity management, the intraday timing risk inside a single day, and the metrics (sources and uses, structural ratios, cash capital) used to monitor it. Reading 70 dissects how dealer banks like Bear and Lehman actually died, through counterparty runs, and Reading 71 designs the liquidity stress scenarios, idiosyncratic versus systemic. The arc: define liquidity risk, see it build, manage it, then watch it kill a dealer bank.",
  "b4-72": "This block turns liquidity analysis into action and funding. Reading 72 turns stress-test results into reports and limits, and Reading 73 is the contingency funding plan, the playbook for when funding disappears. Readings 74 and 75 cover the liability side: pricing deposit services, including conditional pricing and Truth in Savings, and managing nondeposit liabilities through the funding gap and the wholesale funding menu. The through-line: once you can measure liquidity risk, you plan for its worst case and manage the liabilities that fund you.",
  "b4-76": "This block covers the funding and rate-risk machinery around the balance sheet. Reading 76 is repo mechanics, collateral, and the 2008 repo run that froze short-term funding. Reading 77 charges each business for the liquidity it consumes through liquidity transfer pricing. Reading 78 explains the cross-currency basis, why covered interest parity broke after 2008. Reading 79 is interest-rate risk in the banking book, the repricing and duration gaps that trade net interest income against the economic value of equity. Reading 80 closes on illiquid assets, where smoothed returns bias measured alpha and create an illiquidity premium. The arc: fund the book, price the funding, and manage the rate risk it carries.",
  "b5-81": "This block reframes portfolios as bundles of factors and then measures and evaluates them. Readings 81 to 83 are the factor lens: assets are exposures to factors that pay a premium for bad-times risk, the named factors are value, momentum, size, and volatility, and alpha is benchmark-relative, with the low-risk anomaly defying CAPM. Reading 84 turns signals into positions, and Readings 85 and 86 decompose and budget portfolio risk through marginal, incremental, and component VaR. Reading 87 sets up risk monitoring and the risk-management unit, and Reading 88 is the performance-evaluation toolkit, Sharpe, Treynor, Jensen, the information ratio, and time-versus-dollar-weighted returns. Readings 89 to 91 turn to hedge funds, the biases in their reported returns, the due-diligence checklist, and the red flags that predict manager fraud. The arc: understand what drives returns, build the portfolio, budget its risk, then judge and vet the managers.",
  "b5-92": "This block is the current-issues capstone, recent events tested against everything before. Reading 92 dissects the SVB failure, concentrated deposits, held-to-maturity losses, and supervision that arrived too late, and Reading 93 covers the Credit Suisse CoCo wipeout, where AT1 bonds were zeroed while equity survived, upending the usual seniority. Readings 94 to 96 are artificial intelligence in banking: how supervisors use and supervise it, explainability and model-risk discipline for black-box models, and the NIST govern, map, measure, manage framework. Readings 97 to 99 are climate risk, how physical and transition drivers feed traditional risk types, how to measure exposure despite data gaps, and the supervisory principles that follow. Readings 100 and 101 close on crypto and digital resilience, familiar risks in unfamiliar wrappers and the policy tools for tech-driven fragility. The through-line: the exam's frameworks applied to the failures and technologies now in front of regulators.",
  c27: "This block covers portfolio credit risk built from one shared idea: a single-factor model where each firm's fortunes depend partly on a common market factor and partly on its own private shock. Reading 27 builds that single-factor Gaussian copula model from scratch, showing how default correlation reshapes the tail of a credit portfolio's loss distribution without changing its expected loss. Reading 28 takes that exact machinery and applies it to a tranched pool, showing the single most tested asymmetry in this material: rising correlation hurts the senior tranche but helps the equity tranche, because correlation polarizes outcomes toward almost no defaults or mass defaults. Reading 29 recaps the default probability toolkit, introduces CVA and DVA, and restates the same one-factor Gaussian copula in its time to default form, the identical formula to Vasicek's WCDR from Reading 26. Together the three readings are one story: correlation is modeled once, then shown to redistribute risk across tranches, then reapplied to price counterparty default risk over time.",
};
